"Local rank transform: Properties and applications - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167865511000420)\nciteturn9401search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 15.4 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; # Local rank transform: Properties and applications ... In this paper we discuss about some interesting properties of local rank transform and also about some possible applications of this transform such as in edge extraction and image enhancement.An interesting convergence property on the iterative application of this transform on a signal has also been proved in this work. ... Furthermore, we propose a nonlocal and global optimization model to further improve the HR image quality.\n\n## Pattern Recognition Letters\n\nVolume 32, Issue 7, 1 May 2011, Pages 1001-1008\n\n# Local rank transform: Properties and applications\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.patrec.2011.02.005Get rights and content\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn this paper we discuss about some interesting properties of local rank transform and also about some possible applications of this transform such as in edge extraction and image enhancement. An interesting convergence property on the iterative application of this transform on a signal has also been proved in this work.\n\n### Research highlights\n\n► The work discusses about definition and properties of Local Rank Transform (LRT). ► It introduces the concept of-LRT and its uses in developing image processing algorithms. ► The work presents new algorithms of edge extraction and color image enhancement based on LRT.\n\n## Introduction\n\nRank transforms (Conover and Imam, 1981) are often used in statistics in regression analysis and hypothesis testing. In image processing local ranks of pixels have been used in different image operations specially, in filtering impulsive noise (Abreu and Mitra, 1995). Transformation of pixels to their local ranks is referred in this work as Local Rank Transform (LRT). This transform space is not new to the researchers and there are earlier reports on application of these transforms in pattern matching (Banks et al., 1999, Banks et al., 2001, Wang, 2004), and face recognition (Lahdenoja et al., 2006, Smeraldi, 2002). In this work, we would like to elaborate more on the properties of rank transforms or Local Rank Transforms as referred here. We would also demonstrate some possible applications of these transforms in edge extraction and image enhancement. One of the advantage of rank transform is its low computational requirement as the basic operation required for implementing this transform is the comparison operation.\n\n\n## Local rank transform: definition and properties\n\nIn the following we define some of the terminologies and notations used in presenting the local rank transform. Mostly we restrict our discussion in 1-D. However, many of these definitions and properties could easily be extended to 2-D or higher dimension.\n\nDefinition 2.1 Rank of an element\n\nLet \\mathcal{S} be a total ordered set and x is an element of \\mathcal{S}. The rank of x with respect to \\mathcal{S} is defined as the number of elements less than x and it is denoted as r(x\\text{;}\\mathcal{S}). This implies that:where |\\mathcal{S}| denotes the cardinality of the\n\n## Rank transform for noisy images\n\nOne may observe that the LRT is very much sensitive to presence of noise in the data. That is why one may require to perform smoothing or low-pass filtering operation before applying the transform. Interestingly, one may extend the definition of local rank to make the transform more suitable in a noisy environment.\n\nDefinition 3.1\n\n## δ-Rank of an element\n\nLet \\mathcal{S} be a total ordered set and x is an element of \\mathcal{S}. The δ-rank of x with respect to \\mathcal{S} is defined as the number of elements less than x by at least δ amount and it is denoted as {r}_{\\delta }(x\n\n## Applications\n\nOne of the advantage of LRT is its low computational requirement. The transform is carried out in O(n) time and storage complexities. Moreover, as the basic operations are comparisons and counting, the computations are performed using integer arithmetic only. We consider the typical application scenario would be with low-computational resources such as in hand held devices like PDAs, Cell phones etc. In following two subsections we demonstrate two typical applications of image operations,\n\n## Conclusion\n\nIn this work, we discuss about some of the properties and applications of the local rank transform (LRT) of signals and images. With the precise mathematical definition of local ranks, we show that the iterative applications of transform leads to the convergence. The definition is further extended for handling noisy data and called in this work as δ-local rank transform (δ-LRT). The δ-LRT is found to be useful in extracting edges and enhancing images. The advantage of operating with this\n\n## Acknowledgements\n\nThe author gratefully acknowledges both the anonymous reviewers for their insightful comments and suggestions for improvisation of this work.\n\n## References (11)\n\n  * Abreu, E., Mitra, S., 9-12 May, 1995. A signal-dependent rank-orderd mean (sd-rom) filter – a new approach for removal...\n  * J. Banks et al.\n\n## Cited by (17)\n\n  * ### Robust first quantization matrix estimation based on filtering of recompression artifacts for non-aligned double compressed JPEG images\n\n2018, Signal Processing Image Communication\n\nCitation Excerpt :\n\nA formal definition of the three re-compression artifacts is given below for completeness: The residual noise as defined in Section 2 is filtered by applying local rank transform (LRT) [23,24], which is a non-parametric approach of filtering the DCT histogram as described below: Once the residual noise has been filtered, the next step would be to filter the blocking artifact induced error which is described next.\n\nShow abstract\n\nThis paper presents a novel method for first quantization matrix estimation for non-aligned double JPEG compressed images. The non-aligned double JPEG scenario poses several challenges in the form of recompression artifacts which needs to be handled effectively in order to arrive at accurate first quantization estimate. One such recompression artifact which is prominent in the non-aligned scenario is the blocking artifacts arising due to the misalignment of the DCT grids in the successive compression cycles. The proposed method investigates the impact of the blocking artifacts induced errors on the DCT histogram and counters these errors via a novel DCT histogram filtering strategy. In addition, the residual noise which is also a recompression artifact is countered utilizing the local rank transform which adaptively filters the residual noise effects. The filtered histograms are compared with a synthetically created ideal second quantization matrix to estimate the degree of similarity between the two cases. Utilizing the maximum degree of similarity, the selection of first quantization value is performed. Experimental analysis utilizing several non-aligned double compressed JPEG images taken from UCID and RAISE datasets with Quality Factor (QF) of second compression being greater than the first compression (Q{F}_{2}>Q{F}_{1}) along with the comparative analysis with one of the state-of-the-art method shows accurate estimation of the first quantization values for the proposed method. The proposed method finds its application in image forensics as well as in steganalysis.\n\n  * ### Combining sparse representation and local rank constraint for single image super resolution\n\n2015, Information Sciences\n\nCitation Excerpt :\n\nRecently, local rank transform, as a useful tool for describing the data distribution characteristics, is usually used in statistical analysis [7], object detection [14], image denoising [1] and stereo matching [2]. In this paper, considering that local rank can extract better edge information and is insensitive to noise than other edge operator [21], we develop the local rank edge constraint and introduce it into the linear combination stage for SISR task. By combining this edge constraint and the sparse representation, we propose a new SISR method, which can better reconstruct HR image with less edge artifacts and sharp edges.\n\nShow abstract\n\nSparse representation based reconstruction methods are efficient for single image super resolution. They generally consist of the code stage and the linear combination stage. However, the simple linear combination has not considered the image edge constraint information of image, and hence the classical sparse representation based methods reconstruct the image with the unwanted edge artifacts and the unsharp edges. In this paper, considering that the local rank is able to extract better edge information than other edge operator, we propose a new single image super resolution method by combining the sparse representation and the local rank constraint information. In our method, we first learn the local rank of the HR image via the traditional sparse representation model, and then use it as the edge constraint to restrict the image edges during the linear combination stage to reconstruct the HR image. Furthermore, we propose a nonlocal and global optimization model to further improve the HR image quality. Compared with many state-of-art methods, extensive experimental results validate that the proposed method can obtain the less edge artifacts and sharper edges.\n\n  * ### Deep Inception-Residual Laplacian Pyramid Networks for Accurate Single-Image Super-Resolution\n\n2020, IEEE Transactions on Neural Networks and Learning Systems\n\n  * ### Automated microaneurysms detection from retinal fundus images using pixel intensity rank transform\n\n2020, Biomedical and Pharmacology Journal\n\n  * ### Deep pyramid generative adversarial network with local and nonlocal similarity features for natural motion image deblurri